<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-ws-05-supervised-coding">
  <title>Worksheet 5: Supervised Project Coding</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li>Apply the complete coding workflow to approved project responses.</li>
      <li>Transfer calibration skills to authentic data without weakening privacy or provenance controls.</li>
      <li>Produce a locked independent record before receiving response-specific review.</li>
      <li>Use research-lead feedback to diagnose coder drift, artifact gaps, and remaining competency limits.</li>
      <li>Document the scope of any authorization for production coding.</li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This worksheet is the fifth milestone in the researcher training sequence. It
      tests whether a researcher who has completed calibration can apply the full
      method to approved project responses. Unlike the constructed onboarding set and
      the calibration set, these records come from the project corpus and must be
      handled under the data-governance rules in
      <xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Supervised coding is not informal practice with real data. The coder completes a
      small, assigned batch under the same evidence, versioning, and preservation rules
      expected in later production work. The research lead then reviews the complete
      path from question context through disposition, not only whether the final code
      matches a preferred result.
    </p>

    <p>
      The assigned responses must not belong to the held-out validation set. The purpose
      of this milestone is development and transfer assessment. Feedback may be used to
      improve the coder, the handbook, the codebook, the question frame, or the protocol
      before production authorization is granted.
    </p>

    <remark xml:id="rem-ws-05-status">
      <title>Milestone status</title>
      <p>
        Completing the assigned batch does not by itself authorize production coding.
        The research lead must review the preserved record and issue a dated decision
        stating the approved scope, required secondary review, remaining limitations,
        and governing versions.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-overview">
    <title>Milestone at a Glance</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-05-overview">
      <title>Purpose and output of the supervised-coding milestone.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="22%"/>
        <col width="78%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Item</cell><cell>Description</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Primary purpose</cell><cell>Test transfer of the complete method from calibration to authentic project responses.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Work mode</cell><cell>Independent full coding followed by research-lead review and documented feedback.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Data</cell><cell>A small approved project batch that is excluded from held-out validation.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Available support</cell><cell>Frozen analytic materials, approved project systems, and procedural questions to the research lead before response-specific review.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Primary deliverables</cell><cell>A locked coding batch, work-session log, issue log, review record, correction layer, and authorization decision.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Next milestone</cell><cell>Production coding within the authorization boundaries recorded by the research lead.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-prerequisites">
    <title>Prerequisites and Assigned Reading</title>

    <p>
      Begin only after the research lead has documented successful calibration or an
      explicitly limited supervised-coding authorization. Review:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-coding-before" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-coding-pass" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="sec-coding-checklist" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-coding-workflow" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-decision-types" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-edge-case-playbook" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="sec-disagreement" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-edge-cases-decisions" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-practice-readiness" text="title"/> and <xref ref="sec-practice-feedback" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-coding-methodology" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-app-coding-sheet-fields" text="title"/> in <xref ref="app-coding-sheet" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-app-protocol-workflow" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-review" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="sec-app-protocol-final-check" text="title"/> in <xref ref="app-protocol-summary" text="title"/>.</p></li>
      <li><p><xref ref="sec-data-access" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-data-files" text="title"/>, <xref ref="sec-data-reproducibility" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="subsec-data-incident" text="title"/> in <xref ref="ch-data-management" text="title"/>.</p></li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      Review only the question-specific codebooks, vocabularies, frames, and normative
      references assigned to this batch. Do not assume that a familiar code or rule has
      the same scope in a new question.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-access">
    <title>Materials and Access Rules</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-05-access">
      <title>Resources available and withheld during supervised coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>You may use</cell><cell>You must not use</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The approved project system and assigned response batch</cell><cell>Personal copies, screenshots, exports, note applications, or unapproved storage</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The exact question contexts, goals, normative references, and stable identifiers supplied with the batch</cell><cell>Question materials from another version or a similar-looking task</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The assigned frozen handbook, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, and coding schema</cell><cell>Draft, unlabeled, older, or privately edited analytic materials</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Your own calibration notes that do not contain copied project responses</cell><cell>Another coder's response-specific decisions before your record is locked</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The research lead for access, missing-material, identifier, and governing-rule questions</cell><cell>Adjudicated answers or preferred codings before the independent submission</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Pipeline output only when the packet explicitly defines a separate tool-review phase</cell><cell>Machine proposals during an ordinary independent supervised pass</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Approved institutional communication channels</cell><cell>External AI, search, translation, transcription, or cloud services without specific project approval</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-05-real-data">
      <title>Project responses remain protected data</title>
      <p>
        Do not copy a response into a personal document, email, chat, screenshot, or
        external service. Use stable project identifiers in notes and communicate only
        the minimum response text required through the approved channel.
      </p>
    </warning>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-05-review-timing">
      <title>Complete the assigned independent unit before response-specific review</title>
      <p>
        Unless the packet deliberately divides the batch into smaller locked sub-batches,
        do not request or receive item-by-item corrections while the independent unit is
        still open. Early feedback can anchor later records and weaken the transfer evidence.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-packet">
    <title>Check the Supervised-Coding Packet</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-05-packet-manifest">
      <title>Supervised-coding packet manifest.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="34%"/>
        <col width="66%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Required item</cell><cell>Value or confirmation</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Batch identifier</cell><cell>Record the stable supervised-batch ID.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Corpus role</cell><cell>Confirm that the records are approved development or supervised-coding data and are not held-out validation cases.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Authorization record</cell><cell>Record the calibration decision authorizing this milestone and any limitations.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question and response identifiers</cell><cell>Confirm stable IDs, complete response text, and the assigned response count.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Question materials</cell><cell>Record each prompt, representation, goal list, question-frame version, and normative-reference version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Analytic materials</cell><cell>Record handbook, protocol, codebook, vocabulary, and schema versions.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Storage and access</cell><cell>Confirm the approved project location, device, account, and communication channel.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Independent unit</cell><cell>Record whether the batch is locked as one unit or as pre-specified sub-batches.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Review plan</cell><cell>Record who reviews the batch, which records are reviewed, and when response-specific feedback becomes available.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Work-session requirements</cell><cell>Record required timing, sitting, interruption, and fatigue fields.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Advancement rule</cell><cell>Record the competency evidence and authorization decision the research lead will produce after review.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

    <warning xml:id="warn-ws-05-stop-packet">
      <title>Stop when context, authorization, or provenance is incomplete</title>
      <p>
        Do not begin when a response lacks its question context, stable identifier,
        governing versions, approved storage location, or documented corpus role. A
        private repair would create an unauditable project record.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-fields">
    <title>Fields for This Milestone</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-05-fields">
      <title>Fields completed and reserved during supervised coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="31%"/>
        <col width="18%"/>
        <col width="51%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Field or field group</cell><cell>Action</cell><cell>Supervised-coding rule</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Response identity, screening, and routing fields</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Use stable supplied identifiers and the exact allowed values in the assigned schema.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Episode content and relation fields</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Preserve verbatim evidence, missing components, minimal links, and every required relation.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Matching, correctness, coverage, confidence, and notes</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Apply the assigned working codebook and normative reference without conflating local correctness and response-level completion.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Provenance and session fields</cell><cell>Complete</cell><cell>Record coder, timestamp, versions, batch, and required work-session information.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Issue log</cell><cell>Complete when needed</cell><cell>Record data, identifier, artifact, policy, ambiguity, OOV, no-match, and suspected-drift issues without altering source records.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Research-lead review fields</cell><cell>Leave blank during independent coding</cell><cell>The reviewer records confirmations, corrections, disagreement types, rationales, and follow-up in a separate layer.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Correction layer</cell><cell>Complete after review when assigned</cell><cell>Record the reviewed value and explanation without replacing the locked independent record.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>Machine proposal and tool-review fields</cell><cell>Leave blank unless explicitly authorized</cell><cell>A separate tool-review phase must preserve both the independent human record and the computational proposal.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-procedure">
    <title>Supervised-Coding Procedure</title>

    <ol>
      <li><p><em>Verify authorization and packet integrity.</em> Confirm the corpus role, stable identifiers, versions, approved systems, and review plan.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Open only the assigned records.</em> Do not browse neighboring responses, other coder folders, or unassigned exports.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Read the complete question context.</em> Verify the supplied state, goals, and normative information before interpreting the response.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Apply the ten-step workflow.</em> Complete screening through routing in order and preserve the evidence supporting every difficult decision.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Record work conditions.</em> Use the assigned session log and stop when fatigue, interruption, or uncertainty threatens data quality.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Escalate stewardship problems immediately.</em> Stop affected work for possible exposure, unapproved storage, corrupt files, or identifier conflicts.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Log analytic problems without requesting an answer.</em> Continue unaffected records when possible and preserve ambiguity, alternatives, OOV, no match, and policy questions.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Perform the independent integrity check.</em> Confirm required fields, versions, stable IDs, notes, and preservation of source evidence.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Lock the independent unit.</em> Submit the batch or pre-specified sub-batch before response-specific review begins.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Participate in supervised review.</em> Examine the earliest decision behind each correction and distinguish coder error from an artifact or policy problem.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Record corrections separately.</em> Do not overwrite the independent row. Link every review action to the original response, episode, reviewer, rationale, and versions.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>Complete the transfer assessment.</em> Summarize strengths, recurring difficulties, drift indicators, unresolved limits, and recommended production boundaries.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-assignment">
    <title>Assignment</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-manifest">
      <title>Part A: Complete the Packet and Access Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Record the batch manifest, confirm the approved project location and corpus
          role, and report any missing or inconsistent item before opening a response.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-independent">
      <title>Part B: Complete the Independent Full-Coding Pass</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Apply all ten workflow steps to every assigned response. Use one episode row
          per episode and preserve a response-level record when no complete episode is
          present. Complete all authorized analytic and provenance fields.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-issues">
      <title>Part C: Maintain the Issue Log</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each problem, record the response and question IDs, the exact affected
          decision, visible evidence, governing reference, immediate action, and needed
          review. Distinguish analytic uncertainty from privacy, access, identifier,
          version, or file-integrity problems.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-session">
      <title>Part D: Complete the Work-Session Log</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Record every required start and stop time, interruption, and fatigue note.
          When your interpretation of a rule appears to shift, record suspected drift
          and notify the research lead rather than revising earlier rows silently.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-integrity">
      <title>Part E: Perform the Independent Integrity Check</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Confirm stable IDs, exact versions, complete authorized fields, specific
          notes for low confidence, visible alternatives, unchanged source evidence,
          and approved storage. Resolve clerical omissions before locking; preserve
          substantive revisions for the review layer.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-lock">
      <title>Part F: Lock the Independent Record</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Submit the complete independent unit through the approved location. Record
          its identifier and lock timestamp. Do not reopen it for response-specific
          revision after the supervised review begins.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-review">
      <title>Part G: Complete the Supervised Review</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each reviewed difference, identify the earliest affected decision,
          preserve the original value, record the reviewer-supported value, classify
          the disagreement, and state whether the source is coder application, coder
          drift, unclear materials, missing vocabulary, incomplete normative context,
          or a genuine unresolved case.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-corrections">
      <title>Part H: Record Corrections and Follow-Up</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Enter assigned corrections in the separate review layer. For recurring
          difficulties, record targeted remediation or an artifact proposal. Do not
          recode the entire batch unless the research lead issues a documented recoding
          instruction.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ws-05-authorization">
      <title>Part I: Complete the Production-Authorization Record</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The research lead records one of the review outcomes below, the evidence
          supporting it, approved question families or decision types, required review
          level, remaining limitations, governing versions, and effective date.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-questions">
    <title>Questions, Problems, and Escalation</title>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-05-questions">
      <title>Required action during supervised project coding.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="34%"/>
        <col width="66%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Situation</cell><cell>Required action</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A response or metadata contains identifying information</cell><cell>Do not copy it into notes. Continue only inside the approved system and report the issue when the assigned procedure requires masking or review.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A file, identifier, or question context is missing or corrupt</cell><cell>Stop the affected record and contact the research lead. Do not reconstruct the missing content privately.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>A version label is absent or conflicts with the packet</cell><cell>Stop the affected coding and obtain the assigned version. Do not use the most recent file by assumption.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>You need a governing rule</cell><cell>Ask the research lead using stable IDs and the exact decision. Do not request another coder's answer.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>You are uncertain about one response</cell><cell>Record the supported readings, evidence, confidence, and route. Continue unaffected cases when possible.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>The same difficulty recurs across responses</cell><cell>Collect the stable IDs and classify the earliest decision. Propose review of the relevant artifact or competency.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>You suspect your coding has drifted</cell><cell>Stop silent revision, record the point at which the shift became apparent, and notify the research lead for recalibration or planned recoding.</cell></row>
        <row><cell>You accidentally see another coder's work or a machine proposal</cell><cell>Stop, preserve the work completed before exposure, and report the incident so the research lead can determine the effect on the batch.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell>A possible privacy or access incident occurs</cell><cell>Stop the affected work and report it immediately under <xref ref="subsec-data-incident" text="title"/>.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-deliverables">
    <title>Deliverables</title>

    <p>
      Submit:
      <ol>
        <li>the completed supervised-coding packet manifest;</li>
        <li>the locked independent coding record;</li>
        <li>the work-session log;</li>
        <li>the issue and escalation log;</li>
        <li>the independent integrity-check confirmation;</li>
        <li>the research-lead review record;</li>
        <li>the linked correction and follow-up layer;</li>
        <li>the transfer and drift assessment;</li>
        <li>the dated production-authorization decision; and</li>
        <li>the version record below.</li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-ws-05-version-record">
      <title>Version information to preserve with the supervised-coding submission.</title>
      <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="minor">
        <col width="35%"/>
        <col width="65%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Field</cell><cell>Entry</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>worksheet_version</c></cell><cell><c>ws-05-supervised-coding-v0.1</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>pass_type</c></cell><cell><c>supervised_project_coding</c></cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>batch_id</c></cell><cell>Record the supplied supervised-batch ID.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>authorization_id</c></cell><cell>Record the calibration or limited-authorization decision.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>coder_id</c></cell><cell>Use the assigned project identifier.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>reviewer_id</c></cell><cell>Use the assigned project identifier.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>handbook_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>protocol_version</c></cell><cell>Record the assigned version.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>codebook_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>vocabulary_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>question_frame_version</c></cell><cell>Record every version represented in the batch.</cell></row>
        <row><cell><c>schema_version</c></cell><cell>Record the coding and review schema versions.</cell></row>
        <row bottom="minor"><cell><c>completed_at</c></cell><cell>Record the final review date.</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-checklist">
    <title>Completion Checklist</title>

    <p>
      Before final review, confirm that:
      <ul>
        <li>the batch contains only approved project responses and no held-out validation cases;</li>
        <li>all work occurred through approved accounts, devices, folders, and communication channels;</li>
        <li>stable identifiers and governing versions are complete;</li>
        <li>question context and verbatim student evidence were preserved;</li>
        <li>missing reasoning was not repaired;</li>
        <li>matching used definitions and question-specific links rather than keywords;</li>
        <li>correctness and goal coverage remain separate;</li>
        <li>low-confidence, OOV, no-match, ambiguous, and unresolved cases contain useful notes;</li>
        <li>the work-session and issue logs are complete;</li>
        <li>the independent record was locked before response-specific review;</li>
        <li>review corrections were stored separately; and</li>
        <li>no identifying information or unapproved copies appear in notes or files.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-ws-05-review-advancement">
    <title>Review and Production Authorization</title>

    <p>
      The research lead reviews procedural integrity, analytic decisions, issue handling,
      work-session evidence, and the pattern of corrections. The review should identify
      the earliest source of each recurring problem rather than treating every wrong
      final code as a matching failure.
    </p>

    <p>
      Possible outcomes are:
      <dl width="narrow">
        <li><title>Authorize production coding</title><p>The coder may work independently within the question families, artifact versions, and review conditions stated in the authorization record.</p></li>
        <li><title>Authorize with required secondary review</title><p>The coder may produce records, but specified decisions, question types, or confidence levels require review before acceptance.</p></li>
        <li><title>Targeted remediation</title><p>The coder completes a focused new set addressing the earliest recurring difficulty before production authorization is reconsidered.</p></li>
        <li><title>Repeat supervised coding</title><p>A new project batch is required when transfer remains unstable, independence was compromised, or suspected drift affects much of the record.</p></li>
        <li><title>Resolve a project-artifact problem</title><p>The team versions a correction to the question frame, codebook, vocabulary, protocol, schema, or packet before evaluating affected coding again.</p></li>
        <li><title>Suspend project-data access</title><p>Access is paused when privacy, security, or stewardship requirements were not followed.</p></li>
      </dl>
    </p>

    <p>
      The authorization is scoped, dated, and versioned. It does not grant permanent
      access to every dataset or permission to alter codebooks, source files, schemas,
      or adjudicated records. Later drift, artifact changes, or new question families
      may require recalibration or renewed supervision.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
